CREW: Survivors of the Shoah, March 21, 1995. Survivor Henry Wellisch, interviewer Myrna Riback, Toronto, Canada, English.

INT: Could you tell me your name please?

HW: My name is Henry Wellisch.

INT: And where were you born, Mr. Wellisch?

HW: In Vienna, Austria.

INT: What year?

HW: 1922.

INT: When in 1922?

HW: Oh, September 22nd, 1922.

INT: What were your parents' names?

HW: My father's name was Emil Wellisch, and my mother's name was your Jolan Wellisch.

INT: What was her maiden name?

HW: Nee Deutsch.

INT: Could you tell me a little about your childhood in Vienna? Did you have any brothers or sisters?

HW: No. I am the only child.

INT: Did you have other family in Vienna beside your parents?

HW: Yes. My mother had her sister and her family. And on my father's side there was also his brother-- two brothers, actually. And there were various other family members, cousins and second cousins. So there was an extended family.

INT: What did your father do for a living?

HW: My father was essentially a small businessman-- small. I would say small.

INT: Did you live in a house or in an apartment.

HW: Apartment.

INT: Did you live in an area that was predominantly Jewish in Vienna?

HW: I would say yes. Many Jewish people living there. I'm not sure predominantly, but there were many Jewish people living there.

INT: Did you come from a religious home?

HW: No, no.

INT: In the area that you lived were there synagogues? Was Vienna a city in which the Jews lived in certain areas or were they pretty well dispersed through the city?

HW: They were dispersed, but the area that we were living in was what you might call a Jewish district, maybe can compare to the Bathurst and to this area here, you know Bathurst and Wilson or Bathurst and Lawrence area where many Jewish people live there but also many others.

INT: What kind of schooling did you have?

HW: Well, I went first to four years of elementary schools-- of elementary school then four years of what is call [GERMAN], which is a kind of a technical high school. And then I left and I continued for another two years at a commercial high school.

INT: Did you work before the war?

HW: Only after Hitler came, I worked as a-- as a cabinet maker's apprentice for about six or eight months.

INT: Can you remember some details from your childhood of what life was like in Vienna?

HW: Well, life was tough, I'll tell you, because I remember as a child starting around, let's say, in 1930s or so, there was a depression in Vienna. And my father had serious financial difficulties. I remember for instance, when I was a small child up to about 1930 or so, we had a maid. And then around 1930 or '31 or so, my mother had to get rid of her maid, and we didn't have been a maid anymore.

And my father had a very elegant store in this one of the main fancy shopping streets of Vienna. That store went-- it didn't go bankrupt but it didn't do well. And my father had to give it up. So for a while he didn't have anything. And then he, after about two years of doing various things, he eventually started another business, I think in 1935 or so. So it was a difficult time.

And it was also from-- there were also-- there was a lot of unrest in Vienna. In 1934, there were two revolutions in Vienna. In February there was a socialist revolution that had tried to overthrow the government. And in July, there was a Nazi coup d'etat where the Nazis start to seize the government. And I remember those things well because, you know, it was such extraordinary occurrences. So I remember those two events very well.

INT: Did they affect your family personally? Did you find any difference from those events in the way you lived?

HW: Well, life-- while life was more or less normal, there was a lot of anti-Semitism in Vienna, you know? And although, like I told you, I was not a-- we were not a religious family at all, but in retrospect, all my friends were Jews, you know, practically all of them, except one or two. One of my colleagues in school was not Jewish.

But we were living in a Jewish area, and looking over my class in my high school class where I went to, to the high school where I went for four years, I think all of my colleagues, with the exception of one, were Jews. So it was a Jewish area. That gives you an idea how many-- how many Jews were living in the area.

INT: Did you find at school, the Jews were treated differently than the non-Jews?

HW: Well since in my class, we were all Jews, we were all treated the same. Most of the teachers were non-Jews, you see, most of the teachers. Then when I went afterwards to the commercial high school, interestingly enough in the commercial high school, one would think they're mostly Jews. They were not. The Jews went into academics. Well, I went to the commercial. In my class of about 40, there were only three or four Jews. And of course, before the Nazis came, we were treated equally. There was a lot of anti-Semitism but, you know, this was not-- it was, you know, more or less normal, more or less.

INT: How did the anti-Semitism show itself?

HW: The anti-Semitism showed itself that we simply moved in different circles. You see what I mean? As far as I remember, I had hardly any non-Jewish friends. You see, all my friends were Jewish, you see? So we didn't have any-- there was not, not that much contact, you see?

I remember, for instance, my father was told-- after Hitler came to power, my father was told by one over his business associates, not the associates, business acquaintances, he said to him, you know, Mr. Wellisch, we have nothing against you. [GERMAN] he said, to him, [GERMAN]. You are a Christian Jew. It's the others that we don't like, the others you know what I mean?

The ones he meant probably ones from east, from the east, you know? In Vienna, there was a lot of prejudice against the so-called [GERMAN], the Jews who came into Vienna from Galicia. Even amongst the Jewish, even amongst the so-called Viennese Jews, they didn't like either, and amongst them were my parents too. My parents didn't like the Jews who came from, from Galicia because these are the people who were-- who were some of them, when they arrived, they had those beards.

And even the Jewish people who were already completely assimilated, they thought that there are these new people coming in and they will bring the anti-Semitism because they look different. They speak Yiddish. And they're going around with those beards and so forth. It's not good. You see what I mean?

There was a lot of-- even my parents-- my parents never did like the people from [INAUDIBLE]. They never did like those people from Eastern Europe, Jewish people.

INT: Once the Nazis came into power, how did life change? What happened?

HW: Oh, drastic. It was like day and night. There is one day in which I will never forget. The Nazis arrived on March the 11, 1938 when, when the Austrian government kind of caved in and the German army moved in and occupied Austria.

But there was so much resentment against Jewish people in Vienna that there were kind of pogroms on the street, you know? People had to scrub the sidewalk and terrible things. Jews were beaten up and their furniture thrown out the window. Terrible things were going on. I can't-- nothing happened to us, I must tell you.

Nothing ever happened to me personally except that one time I was cornered on the street by a few people and they tried to beat me up. But really nothing happened to me. And my mother was accosted a few-- a few times on the street. And told pig Jew, get out of here and stuff like that.

But let me tell you what happened to my father's business. My father had at that time a business selling fodder for animals like chickens and horses and stuff. Because there was still a lot of horse traffic in Vienna. You know, the beer and ice was delivered by horse-drawn traffic in the '30s. And many people have still had chickens you know, in the outskirts. They had chicken farms and my father had a business supplying those people with grain and hay and so forth.

So at that time, in March, the German army still had a lot of horses. So on March the 12th or the 13th, a few days after the Germans arrived, a German sergeant or something arrived at my father's store. He came in and said, is this a Jewish store? My father said yes. So he said to him, look, we need all kinds of stuff for our horses and you have to deliver this and this and this and this all to this address at a certain barracks. And you'll get paid for it.

And my father of course loaded it all up and he had it all delivered. So after that, my father thought if that goes on like this, in about another week or so, all my, my store will be gone. So what he did, he went to one of his Christian competitors and said, listen, I'm prepared to sell you my entire inventory at rock bottom-- at a discount. You want to take it? Well he said yes. So my father sold him the whole stuff. And he was out of business.

So my father was out of business within a week after the-- within a week after the Nazis had arrived. So after that, we realized that we had to leave because there's no way staying-- no sense staying here. You can't get another job or a business under those circumstances. There were terrible things going on in Vienna. You know, like, like I told you before, you could hardly go in the street. I didn't go on the street anymore.

And I withdrew from school after a few weeks because in school, we had to sit on separate benches. There were three or four Jews in the class. We had to sit on a separate bench. And every hour on the hour when the professor came in, he said, there was-- it had to. It was obligatory. The professor said, [GERMAN], and the whole class stood up and said, Adolph Hitler. And we had to stand there and, of course, we didn't do that. But we just stood there.

And it was just not possible to stay like that. So I didn't even-- didn't even finish the school year. By that time it was about April of 1938. I didn't finish the school year. I quit. I never finished. So that was-- that was the situation in Vienna. And we, we tried to leave, of course, but there was no place to go. That was the situation.

INT: How did you go-- how did your family go about getting food and supplies during this time?

HW: Oh, well for the first the few months at that time, my mother was still able to go shopping regularly. But eventually, especially I think in 1939 as the war came closer, there were ration cards were introduced. And Jews could only go shopping after certain hours, and there was not that much left. You know what I mean?

But until that time, the situation was more, to a certain extent, normal. There was-- my mother still had some stores where she had been shopping for many years, non-Jewish stores, because Jewish stores had been-- many of them went out of business. And she had been shopping there for many years, so they, they treated her-- some of them, they treated her nicely. She could still get enough food.

However, what happened was that many people ran out of money so the Jewish community introduced soup kitchens where people used go. Now, we still had enough money because one of my uncles, who was a very wealthy man, had two or three apartment buildings and he was an Argentinian citizen. He was not living in Vienna. He was leaving in Argentina.

At the time, he was living in Switzerland. And since he couldn't take the money anyway out, he-- the money that he collected from the rent, he gave to my father. The money was given to us, so we had enough money. However, to have enough money is not a good thing for people to know. So as a result, my parents also went to the soup kitchen as if they had no money, you know, not to give that away. Because there were all kinds of funny things going on, and it was better do it this way.

So we went once a day. We went with whole pots and so forth and collected that-- the food from a certain soup kitchen. You had to walk about half an hour to go to get to this place where the-- and that soup kitchen actually was supplied by the American-- by the Joint Distribution Committee. I'm sure you've heard the name, The Joint. It was called The Joint.

INT: And who were these people?

HW: What do you mean, who were these people?

INT: The Joint, who were they?

HW: Oh, it's the Joint Distribution Committee. It was an American welfare organization, a Jewish welfare organization. And they supplied the money, you know, as long as it was possible.

INT: When you say there were funny things going on, you said that your parents-- what does that mean?

HW: Well, you couldn't trust anybody.

INT: Not the Jews either?

HW: There was days that-- of course, we had very little connection with non-Jews, but the less you talked about politics or whatever. Your own priority first, the better, you know? You didn't-- you kept to yourself and that was the best thing to do.

INT: What kind of things could happen to you if you talked to the wrong people?

HW: Well, you could be sent to concentration camp. If you say, for like a stupid thing, let's say, the Nazis are gangsters and somebody overheard you on the street, you can get arrested and send to a concentration camp. And some people did. They thought they were still living in the past.

Let's say somebody's store was smashed up. He went to complain to the police, you know? You complain to the police, you're going to get arrested and sent to a concentration camp. You lousy Jew, you go to complain to the police, what's the matter with you? [LAUGHS] You know what I mean?

INT: Did you personally ever see Hitler?

HW: No.

INT: Were there any demonstrations that you were aware of at the time that he was speaking or people from his--

HW: Yes, when he arrived in Vienna, he was addressed the people-- they were-- there were at that time my understanding is half a million people assembled on the main square. There was such enthusiasm when Hitler arrived in Vienna. That was historic, you know? And of course, as a result of that, he decided immediately to annex Austria, you see? There was such enthusiasm.

INT: Were you there at that time? Did you go there?

HW: Oh, I didn't go there.

INT: The Jews weren't--

HW: Are you kidding? I stayed away from the streets as little-- as much as possible.

INT: After a demonstration like that, was there an aftermath for the Jews where the people--

HW: People were beaten up here and there. There was all kinds of sporadic violence. There was sporadic violence. I don't have to tell you about the one day which we shall never forget in my life, which was soon the 10th of November. We used to-- we used to call that the 10th of November, but it's now known as the Kristallnacht.

INT: Tell me a little about that.

HW: We were living right next to a synagogue. You know, like here on the next-- behind the wall there was a synagogue, like wall to wall. So you know, on, on November 8th, 1938, there was this German counselor was shot in Paris by this-- by Grynszpan. Grynszpan. And we knew something terrible was going to happen. And then he died. He died. He was wounded, and he died. We knew something terrible was going to happen but nobody knew what was going to happen.

So on the 10th of November, like I told you, we were living in a Jewish district. In our apartment house, I think there were all Jewish people. You see, to give you an idea. There was not non-- not-- except the concierge, or what do you call it? The janitor was not Jewish. And she was a very nice person. She saved us. Our janitor saved us. Let me tell you-- tell you how about that happened. So we were living, as I said, we were living right next door to a very nice synagogue.

On the 10th of November in the morning, we got up, and we were living on the third floor, on the top floor. And from our living room window, we could overlook the roof of the synagogue. I look out the window, and I see some soldiers walking on the roof, because the roof was a kind of flat roof with some cupolas on it. I see some people are walking on it with steel helmets. I still remember it. They were walking around, and they were inspecting it.

And then they went. And then it went boom and the roof collapsed. And then of course, we heard screaming because you see we were living right next to the synagogue. But on our side of street was the back of the synagogue. The front of the synagogue was on the-- on another street. So we could hear screams. There was a big crowd apparently in front of the synagogue, and they had entered the synagogue, and they smashed everything up.

What happened was on our side of the street, our street was unfortunately closed. The reason why it was closed was they were-- they were some doing-- they were doing some repairs to the sewers. And they closed the street to car traffic. And only pedestrians could get into the street. And the street was partly dug up. So there were no cars and all the crowds that were-- that were in front of the synagogue were on the other-- on the other street.

So what are we going to do? My father said, you know what we're going to do. This is a terrible Jewish district. We're going to visit my wife's-- my wife-- my mother's sister who was living in a suburb, in a, a working class suburb. And there are hardly any Jews living there. So that's what we'll do. So we sneaked out of the house. And I think we took a taxi. You know, there was-- ordinarily, you know, in the city nothing was going on, only in certain selected areas where there were synagogues and some Jewish districts.

But somehow we were able to get a taxi very easily, and we went out, which is quite far away. It's like from here to, I don't know, a few miles away. We arrive at my aunts' house. They were also living in an apartment building. We go up to the first floor and we knock on the door, or my father. No answer. No answer. We knocked again. No answer.

So we went down to see the janitor. And the janitor-- by the way, my aunt was living in their own apartment building. They owned the apartment building. We go down to the janitor and she says, yes, they are home. You are-- you are my mother's sister's husband and the son had been arrested. And they're in the apartment, but they're not opening the door. So we packed up our bags, well, we didn't pack our bags, but we left again.

So but where to should we go? So my father said, you know, I remember I have an acquaintance. He's a very big businessman or whatever he is. And he's lives in a very elegant district, very elegant. And he had said to my father, if ever something happens in your district, you come to us-- come to me. You know, I'll look after you. You come to us because we're living in heavily Jewish district.

So OK, so we packed-- we pack up our-- not our bags. We, we collected ourselves and went to this place. I don't remember, if we even went by streetcar. But I don't remember anyway. And we went to this other place. We come there, and knock on the door. There is no answer. I look at the door and I see there is a paper, a piece-- a little piece of paper taped over the keyhole. And I look at it and it says on it, [GERMAN], which is Gestapo.

So the place was already sealed. In other words, he probably had been arrested and the place had been sealed. So we left. After we left, we went back again. We arrived back at the house.

INT: At your home.

HW: Back at the home. I don't remember at what hour. We must have left about 8:00 or 9:00 in the morning. And we arrived back, I don't know, several hours later. I don't remember the exact time. We arrived at home. On our street in the middle of the street, there is a big pile of Torah scrolls and curtains and all stuff from the synagogue. It was all burning. A big fire, you know, all these Torah scrolls and prayer books, a big pile. It was all burning. But there was nobody there.

So these people had thrown all this stuff on the street. It was burning. So we knocked on the-- by the way, the apartment house door was locked. So we rang the bell. The janitor came. She unlocked the door, and she let us in and she locked the door again. And that was what saved us. What she did was, she locked all the doors.

And then some gang-- some of those gangsters came and said-- that's what we heard afterwards. They rang the bell and said, what's the-- are there Jews living here? And she said-- she was a Christian. She's said, there are no Jews living in this building. Go away, go. Just jump in the lake. So they went away, and she locked the door again. So the door was locked all day long. And that's-- nothing happened in our house.

That is not quite what happened to one of my cousins, who's all their furniture was thrown out the window, you know? Have you ever heard of a thing like this? Their furniture was thrown out the window. You know, it's unbelievable. Anyway so that was-- that was the 10th of November. We used to always refer to it as the 10th of November. So after, after the 20th of November all Jewish businesses had to close and all the stores were smashed and all the synagogues were burned and smashed. It was just a disaster.

INT: What street did you live on? What was the name of your street?

HW: [NON-ENGLISH]

INT: Was this when your parents decided that it was time to leave?

HW: Oh, my parents decided immediately it was time to leave when my father lost his business. My father realized immediately. But there was no place to go.

INT: So what did they do?

HW: What did they do? As I told you, for instance, my father had a brother who was a very wealthy Argentinian citizen. Now, my father's brother had heard that you could go to Bolivia if you deposited 1,000 USD in a bank there for certain time. So he did that. He deposited-- you couldn't go to Argentina, by the way, because Argentina was closed. OK? Now, Bolivia, apparently, it was possible to go if you had the $1,000.

So, he deposited $1,000 in a Bolivian bank. And we were supposed to go to Bolivia. Then suddenly, the Bolivian government changed the law and that whole thing was cancelled. So we didn't go to Bolivia. In the meantime, one of my cousins who had made it to, to United States-- now that was-- that was about six months-- you know, that was-- the Nazi takeover was in March 1938.

One of my cousins immediately got somehow to the United States. And she went to see a complete stranger. And these particular stranger gave-- made out what is called an affidavit. You know what an affidavit is? OK.

I got an affidavit by this a Mr. I think, Mr. Kaplan from Boston. I had all the documents. I know how much money he had because he had to fill how much-- what you own and what you are. I think it was a Mr. Kaplan from Boston who gave this affidavit for me-- only for me, not for my parents because there was a difficulty with my parents.

As you know, the United States has a quota-- had a quota system. The quota system is based on the place where you are born. Now I was born in Vienna. My mother was born in Austria. My father, by accident, was born in what is now Poland, although he hated the Polish Jews so much. He could never forget that he was born in Poland himself. You know, that was-- he could never get over-- all his life he, who had hated the Polish Jews so much, he was himself a Polish Jew, you know? [LAUGHS]

But it was a pure accident because his family is from Hungary, but his grandfather at one time was residing for a short time there, so that's how he was born in Poland. But the problem was because he was born in Poland, he, he fell under the Polish quota. The Polish quota was over-fulfilled, fulfilled for about 10 years. You see what I mean? The Polish quota for to go to the United States was very small.

The Austrian quota, which was added to the German quota, was about 30,000 people a year. So I could-- if I have an affadavit, I could go within a few months or my mother also. But my mother wouldn't go without my father. So we had to go somewhere else, but there was no-- no-- there was no place to go, absolutely no place to go. So--

INT: Go ahead.

HW: So we tried other things. There was a question of going with a kinder transport to England, but I was a little bit too old, I think, for that. Anyway I was on the list. And then I was kicked off the list. And then, then eventually the thing came from my-- my affidavit came, but then I had to wait. You know, you get an affidavit. Then you had to wait for your medical examination.

INT: Could me stop there for a moment, Mr. Wellisch?

HW: OK.

INT: Thank you.

HW: [LAUGHS]
INT: [INAUDIBLE] arrived from America.

HW: My affidavit, I can't give you exact dates and so forth, when this arrived. Because I was in the meantime, I was trying to get-- I think I mentioned that to you I was trying to become a cabinet maker. One of my uncles knew a cabinet maker. And I went there and started as an apprentice.

And I stayed there with this cabinet maker for about six or seven months. You see, in the meantime, all kinds of things happened that [INAUDIBLE] happen. I was trying to go to England. That didn't work out.

And I was, for the Youth Aliyah the [NON-ENGLISH] Aliyah. I think I was getting too old, I think. That didn't work out. So, eventually, there was nothing else left for me but to wait for the-- for the affidavit. And I registered with the American consulate. And that takes-- that took time.

You see, there were thousands and thousands of people who had also affidavits. And there were only so many doctors at the American consulate. Because the Americans were quite strict, you know, with tuberculosis and stuff like that. And I was waiting. I was waiting.

Now, in the meantime, what else happened? Yeah. By the way, I'd also joined one of the Zionist organizations. Because that was the thing to do. And I went even to a camp, a camp in the country.

And one fine day, it's just a funny thing. It was a camp and in the-- in the-- in the country. It was a Jewish youth organization called Blau Weiss, which was blue white. And in Hebrew, it was called [NON-ENGLISH]. I think it's associated with a labor party, you see.

And one fine day, two SS men came to inspect the local library. One of them was a Mr. Eichmann. He-- because he was the Jewish expert. So he looked at all the books that were in the library. Somebody said, Herr Eichmann-- Herr Eichmann is here to inspect the library. So we-- I think I met-- I think I saw him once when he was there. That's all. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Anyway, so then what happened next? so 1938 went into 1939. Then Czechoslovakia was occupied, where my aunt-- where we had many relatives, and then the summer of 1939. And then, of course, on September the '39, in September of 1939, the war broke out.

But even at that time it was still possible to emigrate to the United States. Because the United States was neutral. And you could travel to the United States via Holland. That was the way to go, the Dutch ships going to the United States from Holland.

So I was still waiting. I was still waiting for the-- for the-- my-- my letter from the American consulate. But what happened then next was this. Then the-- the war broke out, and Poland was overwhelmed, of course, in a couple of-- in a few weeks.

What happened next was the following thing. I got a letter from the Jewish community council-- not a letter, a postcard-- that I have to appear with 50 kilo luggage for transportation to Poland. That was in-- in-- that was in-- end of September or early of October of 1939. Now, we were really stunned, only I, not my parents.

And, apparently, as I found out later on, only it was mostly young man who got this thing. As we found out later, it was supposed to be-- they wanted to send a few thousand. They wanted to-- I think the Nazis wanted to establish a Jewish reservation in the Lublin area. And they were going to send a few thousand people ahead to build all those camps and stuff like that.

But I didn't want to go to Poland, even though there was no question of Holocaust or anything like this. But somehow I didn't want to go to Poland with a mass transport. Who knows what's going to happen there? I don't know.

So, of course, I didn't want to go. My parents didn't want to go. Nobody wanted to go. But what do you do? You have this letter. If you don't show up, the-- the-- if you-- there's a list.

It was all organized. The Jews were told to collect. You see, I got the letter not from the Gestapo.

I got a letter from the Jewish community council. But the Jewish community council was ordered to do that. So if the-- if the Jewish community didn't deliver me on that particular day, I would be picked up by the police. Because they would have to hand my name over to the police that I didn't show up.

So what happened was my parents were running around. My father and I were running around. Is there any way of going quickly out? Because I can't wait for the American visa anymore.

So we found out at that time, which was in October or November, that there are these illegal transports going to Palestine, illegal transports. So I-- my father-- and there are only a certain number of places left on this transport. So we found out that there are few places left from the Mizrachi Organization.

You know the Mizrachi? It's the religious Jewish, religious Zionist organization. They had about four or five places left. And if you pay them, pay the Mizrachi 2,000 marks, you're gone.

So we went to the Mizrachi. The man looks at me. There were all these religious Jews there. We, of course, not religious. But-- he says to me, you [NON-ENGLISH] every day? I, of course, I lied like a-- like a-- I lied. I said, of course, every day. I don't even know what that was.

I know what it was, but I didn't, you know? We were not religious at all. We didn't go to-- never go to the synagogue. Although we are living next to a synagogue, I never went to that synagogue, except when a-- when a-- if somebody got married or something.

Anyway, the fact is, I got all the paperwork was done within a week or so. I get my passport with the exit permit with a false visa to Paraguay, which is in my passport even today. And I left Vienna for Bratislava.

INT: By yourself?

HW: By myself. By myself with a few other young men. We had about three or four other people on the train for Bratislava, which was at that time-- Czechoslovakia didn't exist anymore. But there was an independent state of Slovakia, which was like a German satellite state.

And these transports were being assembled in Bratislava. And then we're supposed to go down the Danube to Romania, and from there by ship to-- to Palestine. There was no Israel, of course, at the time.

And so I arrived in Bratislava. And we were picked up and brought to this camp, where there were already about 200 or 300 people mostly from Vienna, who were also waiting for that transport to get organized. Now, that was-- I left Vienna. And I had the date, of course.

I left Vienna on December 23, 1939, a day just before Christmas. I still remember it. I have it stamped in my passport. [LAUGHS] So there's no question about it. So I left Bratislava.

And arrived-- we arrived there. And we stayed there-- I stayed there at the camp waiting for the transport to get organized. Because we are not just 300.

There was supposed to be 1,000, or I don't know how many people are going-- supposed to go down. Of course, this all was done with the permission of the German authorities. Because they knew I wasn't going to Paraguay with a false visa there.

INT: Why did they allow you to do that?

HW: Well, they want to get rid of the Jews. The policy at that time was not to exterminate the Jews. The-- the idea was to get rid of the Jews, to send them to England, to anywhere to-- to America, just to get rid of them. You see?

INT: What kind of a camp was it that you were staying at Bratislava?

HW: It was-- [LAUGHS] it was an abandoned ammunition factory. And it was-- we were living in this abandoned ammunition factory called the Patronka. And there were about 300 or 400 people there. And it was the winter.

The winter was extremely cold. And the place was very badly heated. Let me tell you. It was so cold, we were sleeping on the floor on straw, by the way. But there was a huge room there. Because it was an abandoned factory.

So it was this huge room, only men in this room. And the women were in another room. It was so cold, I was hanging my-- my winter coat on-- on the window like on a little nail. In the morning it was frozen to the window.

So for what we did the next time, we got some wood, some boards, and we nailed wood across and filled the space between the boards and the window, filled with sawdust. Because it-- the window, some of the panes were broken and stuff like that. So we were able to-- to survive.

But the winter was extremely cold. Now, what happened further with the winter was that the Danube froze. As a result, because you see we were supposed to go on the Danube.

Because on the Danube, if you go on the Danube, in those years the Danube was an international river. And you didn't need any transit visas to go through Hungary, Yugoslavia, Romania, or Bulgaria. As long as you were traveling on the Danube, you didn't need anything. It was an international river that was a legacy of the First World War of the Versailles Treaty, or whatever.

But the Danube froze, so we got stuck in-- in-- in Bratislava, or Pressburg as its known in German. What happened in the meantime, my parents got a letter from the American consulate in-- in January or February of 1940. The letter arrived from the American consulate, I should appear at the American consulate for my medical exam to go to America. But I wasn't in Vienna. I couldn't go back.

So my parents-- there was no American consulate in Bratislava. There was only one in Budapest. But it was difficult to get to Budapest. You'd need a visa. It's impossible.

So my parents went. And my parents knew I was stuck in Bratislava. Maybe we'll never be able to get from there to Palestine.

My parents went to the [NON-ENGLISH], the Jewish community, and-- and explained the situation. And the [NON-ENGLISH], they went to see the Gestapo. And the Gestapo helped them.

The Gestapo sent, allowed, a Jewish functionary from the Jewish community council to go to Bratislava and pick me up. Not only me [INAUDIBLE], but there were also two other people who were in the same position. There were-- we were three young men all about 18-19, to go back to Vienna with the permission of the Gestapo to get the American visa, so we could go to America.

You see, it's very interesting. These-- you don't-- people don't know about those things, the Gestapo helping Jews, you know? Anyway, the Jewish functionary arrived in Bratislava. He-- we picked up our-- we picked our bags-- we packed our bags and went back to Vienna.

But we tried to get back to Vienna. At the German border, we were stopped by the German passport control. He looks at my passport.

And he says, look, with this passport it says here, you can only leave Germany once. And you can never come back. That's what it said there. You see what I mean?

But the functionary said, it says, here, I have the letter from the Gestapo. He say, Gestapo, schmepapo. I am not-- I am not interested in the Gestapo. I am a border passport control. Here, it says in the-- in the passport, you can't come back. And I don't care what the Gestapo says.

You can't come back. I have nothing against you personally. He was very nice, actually. He says, look. I have nothing against you, Jewish, or non-Jewish. It says here you can't come back. So you can't-- you can't come back. Never mind what the Gestapo says.

The fellow from Vienna was allowed to go back to Vienna. Because he hadn't-- he didn't have that stamp in his passport. But we were not allowed to go back to Germany. You know I mean?

So what happened, we were stopped on the border. We were put into a room there. And they made some jokes with us. We had to crawl under the bed.

Some other people came, some nasty Nazis came. They told me to crawl under the bed and start singing. Then he said to me, OK, jump. Start jumping. I had to jump, and then I had to do this and that. But I wasn't beaten up.

I must say, I wasn't beaten up. And then I was sent back to-- so on the same day, I arrived back in Bratislava in the camp again, after my adventure with the-- with the German. After that, I said, I'll never go back to this bloody country again, you know after that experience. [LAUGHS] So that was-- that was-- that was that.

Now that was-- it must have been February or March of 1939. And we were still were stuck in this-- the Danube was by then, unfrozen. But they still were not able to arrange all the ships and everything and so on and so forth.

Then it was summer of 1940. And then France was defeated. And-- and the Germans occupied practically the whole of Europe. But that didn't affect us, actually.

And then I got a letter one fine day from my parents that my parents told me that another transport is going to Palestine, another illegal transport from Vienna, which could-- which is probably in combination with mine. And they are going to join that one, too. By that time, it was late in the summer.

And I got the-- like I said, I got a postcard from my parents. They are definitely going on that transport. And, eventually, that transport was put together by some underground Jewish underground organization, with of course, all with the permission of the Gestapo, all with the permission of Mr. Eichmann and all these people.

And there was a transport of about 3,600 people. Imagine a huge illegal transport leaving Germany in the middle of the war for enemy [INAUDIBLE], for Palestine, with the permission of the Germans. OK?

There were about 900 Jews from Vienna. By the time in Bratislava, we were about 300 in our camp. And there was a second camp in Bratislava with about 400, also all Jews from Czechoslovakia. Then 600 were supposed to come from Danzig.

And-- and some-- and, also, it's I don't know how many hundreds from Germany, for a total of about 3,500 or 3,600 people. And there were all supposed to travel down the Danube on about four-- on four ships. Because you can't get a ship that size.

So there were four ships. One-- the one from Vienna was called the Schonbrunn.I was on the Uranuswith 1,000, the Helios, and the Melk.There were four ships, the Uranus,the Helios,the Melk,and the Schonbrunn.So my parents-- and all of these ships sailed roughly at the same time.

My parents sailed from Vienna on the Schonbrunnwith 900 other people. I sailed from Bratislava with 1,000 people. And the others also came from Danzig. They came by train with a special train with a sealed train from Danzig to Bratislava. They entered the ships at Bratislava.

And that whole convoy of about four ships sailed down the Danube from-- to-- to-- Budapest and all down the Danube through Belgrade, Yugoslavia, Romania, Bulgaria, until all the ships arrived in Romania near the Black Sea.

INT: OK. Can we go back a little bit?

HW: Yeah.

INT: Can you tell me a little bit about the conditions at this camp in Bratislava? Who organized it? How did you get food?

HW: Yes.

INT: Was there medical attention?

HW: OK. I can

INT: Tell me a bit about that.

HW: Sure. [LAUGHS] The-- the camp was, like I said, it was in a disused factory. We were not allowed to go out. We were under the-- under-- under guard of the Slovak-- the so-called Hlinka Guard, which is really like storm-- the Slovak storm troopers.

But they were not too bad. You could pay them off or something like this. But we never-- we were never allowed to go into town, except with special permission. And I had an uncle in-- in Slovakia.

He came one day. And I was allowed to leave the camp for two weeks. And I stayed with my uncle in Slovakia for two weeks. He did not survive the war. He and his wife were sent to Auschwitz.

But, anyway-- and we were in the camp. In the camp, there were two-- [LAUGHS] in the camp, there were two kitchens, a kosher kitchen and a treifa kitchen. When I arrived, they asked me, you're kosher or treifa? So I said, why? Where is the better food? I said, well-- they said, the-- the treifa have more meat. And the kosher have better pastries. So I said, I go for the treifa. [LAUGHS] So I--

So what did they do? They put me into the kosher kitchen, of course. I said, treifa, they put into the kosher kitchen. This is-- this is typical. You know what I mean? [LAUGHS]

So I was in the-- and there, we stayed. I stayed there in this blasted camp from December of 1939, because I arrived in December 1939, till September. I was there for nine months in this camp.

And we, eventually, we did some work. And we-- we worked in the garden. And we did grow some vegetables. But, basically, it was very boring.

INT: What was the morale like? Did people--

HW: The morale was low. Because we-- there was continuously the danger that we would be sent back, and that we would not be able to-- to make it. We were supposed to go to Palestine. And the Slovaks didn't want us to go there.

The Slovaks were paid off by the Join again. The Joint provides the money for our food and everything. The Joint provided that. We didn't know about it. But I found out about it later.

The Joint provided the dollars to-- to-- to have us fed. You see what I mean? That was paid. You know, there is some literature on this, on who-- who-- who supported us.

INT: You were allowed to send letters home.

HW: Yes.

INT: So you-- you were in correspondence with your family.

HW: Yes. I was-- I still have some postcards, I think. I was-- yes. There was-- it was possible to send the letters. I even had some relatives in Bratislava on my mother's side.

And one-- one or two days, I was allowed to visit my relatives in Bratislava. Also, they did not survive. Not one of them survived.

INT: So on what date was it that this convoy of ships started to go?

HW: I have to refresh my memory. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] The-- the convoy left Bratislava on September 3, 1940.

INT: Tell me about your ship.

HW: Well, the-- the ship was a Danube ship. It was a very nice ship, like a paddle wheeler. You know, on the Danube, they had paddle wheelers. And we were-- we were-- it was very crowded, but was very nice.

[INAUDIBLE] the weather was perfect and we were sailing down the Danube, which is very picturesque. And we had a great time. [INAUDIBLE] The food was pretty good. It was very crowded. I was sleeping on deck.

You know. But who the hell cares? I mean, I was a young, young man. It this very crowded and-- and maybe the toilet facilities were not, because it was so overcrowded. You see what I mean?

A ship like this only-- only carries maybe 200 people. We were 1,000. But it was OK. In-- in-- in Bulgaria, the ships were stopped for a certain period of time. And my parents' ship-- and our-- my parents were on another ship, right?

I was on the Uranus.My parents were on the Schonbrunn.The ships were lying next to each other. And I jumped over, and I visited my parents, the first time seeing my parents in nine months.

My mother had, by the way, tried to persuade her sister to join the transport, but she refused. They said, look, we are--- we are retired. We're living in our own house.

And what can happen? What can happen to us? Nothing. Yeah, we are retired. My-- you know, but they didn't survive. Yeah.

INT: How long were you on the ship?

HW: [LAUGHS] Well, the ship sailed from Bratislava on September 3. And it arrived in Tulcea, which is the Danube-- the Danube estuary on September 11. So that's-- it took quite some time to get-- get down the Danube.

And what happened next was there were three high-sea ships standing in the Danube estuary, three Greek ships. But they didn't have the Greek flag. They had the Panama flag, which is like a flag of convenience.

Because it was all illegal. The-- the Greek government did not allow-- did not allow this kind of thing. So they got the ships registered under Panama. Under the Panama flag, you can do anything, practically, you know, even probably today. I don't know.

But anyway, so my parents were on one ship, the Atlantic1,700 people. I was on another ship, the Pacificwith 1,000 people. And there was a third ship, the Milos,with about 900 people.

INT: So they switched you to these ships at that point.

HW: Yes, from the Danube ship. From the Danube ships, we were switched to those ships. And the interesting thing is, the Germans are very clever. These-- these Danube ships, on the way back, they were filled with the so-called Volksdeutsche, who were evacuated from Russia at that time.

Remember, Germany and Russia was at that time allies, right? And the Germans were, the Volksdeutsche, the German-speaking people from Russia, were going back to the-- back in the [NON-ENGLISH], go back-- going back to Germany. So the Jews paid for the-- the-- the repatriation of the Germans from Russia. You see what I mean?

So anyway, we were-- we were-- I was on one ship. My parents were on another ship. Now, what happened next was that my parents went to see our-- the leaders of the transport and said, look, we are on this ship here. Our son is on another ship.

Is it possible to get him transferred from one ship to the other with the permission of the Romanian Port Authority? Because you're not allowed to go back and forth. You know, there were Romanian police boats going around there that nobody should go on land, God forbid. Do you know what I mean?

So they arranged with the Romanian authorities. There was an exchange made. And one fine day, somebody came. Listen, your parents made arrangements to go to the other ship. Pack your bags, and go to the other ship.

So I packed my bags. I had only one small suitcase and a-- and a backpack. And I joined my parents on the Atlantic,on the-- which was the biggest ship with about 1,000 tons with 1,800 people. I mean, the-- the overcrowding is difficult to describe, let me tell you.

INT: Try.

HW: Try? At one time while traveling, it was so crowded that the ship started to lean over to one side. It leaned over. Because it was-- it was-- how shall I put it? There was too much weight near the top. It leaned over to one side.

Now, we had a-- we had a police on board of the ship of our own, like an order group. They were stationed at strategic points to-- to see that the traffic goes and so forth. I was also a member of that group.

And what-- what-- when that-- that happened quite frequently that the ship started to lean over to this side or to this side. So at that time, the order was everybody to the other side. [GERMAN]. The-- the language on board the ship was German. All these Jews were from Central Europe. [NON-ENGLISH]. It means everybody to the left or everybody to the right, whatever necessary. And then the ship slowly righted itself. [LAUGHS]

Also, it was so crowded that two people couldn't pass each other. So as a result, it was organized that if you want to go in this direction on deck, on a certain deck, you could go only in this direction. And the-- and to go back the other direction, you had to go on the other side of the ship. It was, you might say what was one-way traffic.

INT: What were the toilet facilities like?

HW: The toilet facilities? [LAUGHS] The toilet facilities, there was some toilets there, but only for women with small children. The rest of the toilets there was at the stern of the ship, that is to say the back of the ship.

There were about eight toilets that were overhanging the water. They were kind of-- you went to the toilet. It went straight into the water. And there was-- there was on one side, there was a lineup for men. And then on other side, there was a lineup. There was always a lineup. Because everybody had diarrhea.

INT: Why?

HW: Because of the-- the food, and the-- the-- the lack of water. I don't know. Anyway, I had-- everybody had diarrhea. So as a result, there were always lineups. And [LAUGHS] my mother-- my mother was a-- my mother also was an order, order guard.

And for the ladies, because you had to go quickly, you had to say whether you go big or small. And if you went to-- if you want to be small, you had to go small. But some people cheated.

They said they're going small, but they actually went big. And my mother caught one of those. And my mother told me, she said, [INAUDIBLE] I caught this one. She said she was going small, but she went actually big. So she's says-- my mother said, I gave her hell. [LAUGHTER]

So I mean, the-- the-- it was difficult to describe the-- the overcrowding. I mean, some of those pictures that you've seen-- but it's-- it's difficult to describe. Below deck there were-- where I was living, way down below, there were three tiers of bunks.

And some of these people-- I'm telling you the truth. Some of these people like had-- they had diarrhea. And it was dripping. I swear. It was dripping down from the top in some cases. You know what I mean?

People couldn't-- couldn't restrain themselves. That-- that happened. Not very often, but it did happen sometimes. So it was terrible.

And the food was, of course, you had to line up for the food all the time. There were continuous lineups. Because there were-- there was some food. It was very bad. And it was not enough.

And there was not enough water. And there was not enough food. And the food was very bad. For instance, after a while the bread gave out.

And we had biscuits. And I remember, let me tell you, I remember distinctly it was very damp. These biscuits that we had, they turned green.

And I had-- I remember I had a brush, a steel brush to brush the shoes. And I brushed off the green with that stuff, you know, with the brush. And that's where I got the diarrhea, I'm sure, from-- from that stuff.

INT: Was there medical attention for people who got sick?

HW: Yes. There was. There were about five or six doctors there. We had a hospital on-- on board. But during the trip, about 12 people died of typhus. But that was kept secret. We didn't even know about that.

Because, otherwise, there would have been a panic. So nobody said anything. But I found that out. We found that out later on that a few people died of typhus, because of those terrible sanitary conditions.

We couldn't wash. There were no washing facilities. The only way to wash yourself was everybody had a little can made from a-- from a can with a string. I used to throw that overboard and pull the water up from-- but that was salt water, of course, you know.

INT: The only organization that kept order was this Jewish group? Were there any other guards on the ship?

HW: No. There was only the Greek crew.

INT: And how were they?

HW: Oh, they were quite nice. The captain was a crook. But the crew, there was just a few-- a few crew members, not many, maybe 10 or 15. And they kept to themselves. And we had a leadership. They-- they negotiated with the captain everything that had to be negotiated with. [LAUGHS]

INT: Did you feel any effects of the war outside of the ship while you were taking this journey?

HW: Yes. Well, let me now start about the journey. Well, first of all, there were three ships anchored in the estuary of the Danube at a place called Tulcea. You can find--

INT: Can I stop you there for a moment, Mr. Wellisch? Thank you.

HW: Right. Well, that's good place to stop.

INT: Good.
INT: Tell me about the journey.

HW: OK. Now, the Atlantic tried to leave Tulcea, immediately broke down. What happened was the Atlantic broke down, and the other ships-- the two other ships-- that is to say the [? Milos ?] and the-- what was the name of the other ship? The [? Milos ?] and the Pacific left on their own. And there was some-- there was some engine or-- some engine problems. And, as a result, the Atlantic left a few days later or a day or something later on its own.

And without any trouble, we reached Istanbul. And a day later, we went through the Black Sea. Istanbul-- at Istanbul, we were boarded by the Turkish police and we got bread. We always got bread and water from the Jewish community. They supplied us with bread and water. We had some staples like sausage and-- and other stuff, and canned food that was a staple.

But the boat came along with bread and with water. So we got bread and water. Then we continued. And we continued through the Aegean Sea. And, as you know, there are all these Greek islands. We stopped first at, I think, Mytilene, which used to be called, I think, at one time, Lesbos, and at Chios and at Ios and various other small Greek islands.

And whenever we stopped, we got, from the Greek Port Authorities, bread and water. They, of course, knew who we were. We were-- because this was not the first transporter that had gone through. They knew who we were, and they were kind enough to supply us for free, I think, with bread and water.

So the Greeks-- I must say the Greeks, I have high regard for Greek-- for the Greeks since then. They supplied us with food for free. Also, I remember one-- one incident at one of those islands. We had arrived there, and we got the bread and the water, and then we continued on our way.

And as we were sailing out, there was this Greek-- [CRYING] there was this Greek priest standing there, on a-- on the edge of the port. I knew it was a Greek priest because they have a very specific dress. And he stood there-- [CRYING] [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

Anyway, he raised his hands and he kind of-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] he kind of blessed the ship. So, [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS] anyway, we continued through these Greek islands, and arrived at-- we arrived at Heraklion in Crete. We arrived at Heraklion in Crete on October the 16th-- on October 16th.

And then we ran out of coal. Our ship was a coal ship, by the way, and did not have a radio. As I told you, the conditions on the ship were terrible, but I don't have to go through this again. But we ran out of coal, and could continue any further. We did not have enough coal. So we were standing there for several weeks.

In the meantime, what happened was that the war caught up with us. What happened was, on-- on-- I have to check my dates again. On October-- we arrived in Heraklion on October the 16th. On October the 28th, Italy declared war on Greece.

And there were-- every day, there were air raid alarms. Not air raid attacks, there were air raid alarms. But fortunately there were no air raid attacks. We were standing in the-- in the port. I mean, there was nothing-- So the Greek government, of course, did not want us there, because they-- Greek-- Greece is a small country.

They had enough trouble to fight the war against the big power like Italy. So they wanted to get rid of us. So eventually-- eventually-- I don't know what went on behind the scenes-- somehow we got coal. One fine day, a barge arrived with coal, and it was loaded on to the ship and we sailed. We sailed east, in the easterly direction.

So we sailed east, and-- of course the captain, in charge of the ship. We sailed for about a day or two, and then the captain said, we have to go back, because we don't have enough coal. We can't make it to Palestine, or not even to Cyprus or anywhere else. There is not enough coal there.

So-- but we didn't want to go back, you know? Because we didn't want to go back to enemy country, because we were already on our way to Allied territory. But he said we had no coal. What happened was the ship came to a standstill. The ship stopped in the middle of the ocean.

So what do you do? What we did was, our leadership-- most of these people in our leadership, or some of them, were Czech. And one of them was a Czech Air Force officer who know, somehow-- who had a good idea on how to operate a ship.

So, in other words, we arrested the captain and he took over the ship, together with the first Greek-- with the First Officer, who was also a Greek, who decided to cooperate with us. And we continued. However, how do you continue when you have no coal? So what happened then, we decided to break up the ship.

The ship was, to a certain extent, made out of wood. All the decks and the interior partitions, everything was made out of wood. So we started to take all the wood-- we ripped up all the decks and took out all of the partitions-- the cabins, all the-- everything made out of wood was ripped up and cut into little pieces.

And there was a long chain of people going into the engine room. And all those pieces were sent down into the engine and [INAUDIBLE] thrown into the-- into the boiler-- into the furnace. And in this fashion, we were able to continue for a few more days.

We-- the ship was going a little bit, and then it came to a standstill, because there was not enough steam there. Then we chopped up more wood, got it back in, and continued again. And this went on for a few more days until we eventually saw land in the distance.

But just before we did that, during that period-- by the way, our ship did not have a radio. We were-- there were no ways we could-- could communicate with anybody, and there was no way we could get help from anybody. But during those few days when we were in this-- in this situation, we were caught by two destroyers.

Two British Destroyers, fortunately. They were not Italians but British. They came along, and they came with great speed, and they circled around and they [? semaphored ?] with this thing here and said, who are you? So we said back, we are Jewish refugees and we need help.

So they said, I'm sorry, we can't help you. Because they couldn't. I mean, what can they do? But they said they will-- we will-- we will radio that-- that you are in this situation. And they-- and they said goodbye and they took off. [LAUGHS] They took off in a big rush.

And we continued a little bit further until we-- until the wood gave totally out and we were in sight of land. In other words, in the far distance we could see some mountains, and we had no more wood. So we were stopped. And then we were waiting for somebody to come from there and pick us up and tow us in. And that's what happened.

A few hours later, a motorboat came out and it was flying the British flag, so we knew that we had reached-- somebody said we are in Cyprus. That land over there is Cyprus. So we had reached Cyprus. So was-- so it was great excitement. We had reached, finally, the Allied territory. So by that, we had escaped from Nazi Germany. That was-- that was--

INT: What was the condition of the people on the ship at this time? Was there a lot of illness? Was there-- What was the morale like?

HW: No, there wasn't. The conditions were terrible, but people were bearing up. Except, of course, some-- some got sick and died-- about 10 or 12. But they were buried oversea. They were thrown overboard.

And-- and-- but, you know, there was no time to think about things like this. You had to-- you were busy all the time, running up for food or whatever. There was no time to worry about things. You don't worry about things. You just try to keep alive.

And there's no heroism or anything like this. You do-- you take it as it comes, from-- from hour to hour. There's nothing-- you don't worry about what is going to happen tomorrow, because you don't know what's happen-- going to happen in half an hour.

That's all I can tell you. There's no time to think about these things. Philosophizing, you-- there is no time. You-- you just carry on as best as you can. You line up for your food and then you see where you can wash yourself and maybe you can-- maybe you can visit my parents, and then you are on other duty, or you have to stand here for a [? few ?] hours and watch the people don't cross the bridge, because if they cross the bridge they can beat the queue, which is lined up from there.

So there's always something to do. Or then you have to sleep for a few hours at night, or then it rains. Then you have to go under, or you go below. So there's always something to do. That's it. [LAUGHS] What can I tell you? There's no time.

INT: So you reached Cyprus.

HW: Then we reached Cyprus. So the British sent out a-- a-- a-- a tugboat, and they towed us in, because we couldn't do it on our own. We had no-- no engine, and no engine power. So they-- they towed us in to port. The port is Limassol, the big port in Cyprus, and we were--

So the first thing the British was that they arrested the captain and the whole crew, because you know-- because they were considered smuggling illegal immigrants into British territories. So they were immediately arrested, and I don't know what happened to them afterwards. I think they were tried, and they get a few months in jail or whatever. I have no idea.

Then the British said, well, who are you? Of course they knew who we were, because the other two ships had arrived before. Right? So they knew, but they said we are-- we are Jewish refugees from-- from Germany and other countries here.

And they said, well, all right, we'll send you on to Palestine, and you'll get some food. But first of all, for the food you have to pay. See, the Greeks had supplied us with food for free. The British, we had to pay. So, in other words, everybody-- I-- everybody had either one pound-- either-- we were allowed to take out of Germany either one pound or $5.

So I had, I think, one pound and my parents each had $5. That-- I think some of it had to be handed over to pay for the food-- bread and other food-- that-- that we got from the British. They were very stingy, I must tell you. That was-- that-- everybody was very angry about it.

I think even some people had to hand over some of their jewelry. But that I don't-- that I'm not sure about. But I remember we had to hand our-- hand over what little money we had. And also we had a big box of German drugs that we were allowed to take. And these are-- [INAUDIBLE] we handed that also over to the British, which was very valuable, you know?

And then the British supplied us with coal and with a British crew, with a British captain, and coal, and we sailed for Palestine, which is very close from Cyprus to-- to Haifa is just a-- just a day. And we sailed for Palestine. And we saw the-- the promised land [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] in the distance. We saw the port and Mount Carmel in the distance.

Everybody was singing the Hatikvah on deck. It was a great moment. I can assure you that nobody who on that ship will-- [CRYING] Anyway, so we arrived in Haifa, and-- and the British police came on board and they said, well, the other two ships had arrived. But the problem is there is no-- no place on land.

So you have to go to another ship, but you'll be very comfortable there. Now usually, what the British had done in the past, what they had done was they-- people were-- with illegal immigrants, that is to say-- they put them into a camp and then-- then they let them go, but they deducted the number from the monthly immigration quota.

There was a certain number of people that were allowed to go to Palestine. If illegal came, they deducted the-- you know? But nobody was deported. You know? So we figured the same thing was going to happen to us.

But not this time, apparently. What they-- what they said-- the British said, look, there is no space in the camp. You have to go on this other ship. And it-- you will be transshipped from-- because you can't stay on this terrible ship. It's all over crowded and all that.

So always 200 people are supposed to get their things together and you will be transshipped from your ship to this big ship which was-- which was standing on the other side of the port. The ship is called the Patria, a French passenger liner which was confiscated by the British and used as a-- as a transport. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So the first 200 people packed their bags, and they went down on a big barge, and they went over to the-- to the Patria. They went over, then the barge came back empty, and picked up the second 200, and they left for the Patria.

They left for the Patria, but we couldn't really see the Patria. We could see only the three funnels at the other side of the port. And then suddenly, those three funnels disappeared. They were just gone. We didn't hear any sound or explosion or anything.

But what happened then, afterwards, the last group of people who had been transshipped, or were supposed to be, they came back. And they were all crying and said the ship has capsized, and there are all people swimming in the water, and terrible things are happening, and so forth. So we knew that something terrible had happened.

So what happened next was that the British decided, well, we couldn't stay onboard the ship, they're going to-- to send us to a camp after all. So we were taken off the ship, and my father and I went to a camp-- like a detention camp or whatever. A camp with barracks in Acre.

And my mother, for one reason or another, went to a ladies' camp-- a women camp in Atlit, which is just outside of-- of Haifa, if you know the place. And there we stayed for about two weeks. And there we recovered some.

We had-- we had comparatively good food and we were able to take showers and all of that. And we had breakfast and lunch and dinner, which I'm sure it was very plain, but it was very good. Probably British Army food, I would assume. [INAUDIBLE] We really were able to clean ourselves again after being on that ship for about two months or whatever, right?

We arrived-- by the way, we arrived in-- in Haifa-- let me see-- we arrived in Haifa on November the 24th. So we had left Tulcea-- we had left Tulcea on October the 7th of 1940, and we arrived in Haifa on November the 24th. So that's the period we were on that-- on that-- on the terrible ship, on the Atlantic. It's a long time, you know, in those conditions.

So we were in this camp, and we were quite happy there because we figured, you know, OK, we'll stay in the camp. Maybe we'll stay another two months or whatever, and then we'll be discharged. You know? Great. But that was not to be.

The British, for one reason or the other-- political or whatever-- they were fighting alone. You remember France was defeated? They probably didn't want to have any problems with the Arabs. There are 3,500 people come that are illegal, and they-- you know, the political troubles.

They-- they said, well, all these people are going to be deported. As a matter of fact, all these people who had already been on the-- on the Patria, they also were supposed to be deported. But then, because there was this terrible accident, or this-- the Patria capsized, the British government modified their decision.

They said as follows-- those people who had been on the Patria and who had been saved-- 250 died, by the way. 250 drowned. But there were about-- about 1,500 or 1,700 people on the Patria, plus the crew and police and so forth. Those people who had been-- those refugees who had been on the Patria, they could stay in Palestine.

They were going to be pardoned, so to speak. But the 1,700 or so who were on the Atlantic-- who had come on the Atlantic and had not been on the Patria yet, they are still going to be deported. And--

INT: Did you have any contact with the Jewish authorities in Palestine?

HW: Only very little. There were a few Jewish auxiliary police at the camp, and these were the only people we were in touch with. And these Jewish auxiliary police, they were the one who told us that you're going to be deported. And what they told us was that you're going to be deported, and what you should do, you should offer passive resistance.

You shouldn't dress yourself. You should not pack. You should offer passive-- you can't offer active resistance, but at least you should offer passive resistance. So what the-- what the majority of people did, they didn't dress and they-- they stayed naked. They stayed naked. They didn't pack anything.

And so the British made an announcement saying, next day, everybody has to be packed. Breakfast will be served at 5:00 in the morning, and you have to be packed for transportation. They didn't say anything, where or what. They just said you have to be packed. You'll be transported somewhere else.

But we had already heard from the Jewish police that they were going to deport us. Not-- nobody knew where to, but they were going to deport us. And this was very much opposed by the-- by the Jewish people of Palestine, because it was supposed to be the Jewish homeland.

You're supporting Jewish refugees from the Jewish homeland? You know what I mean? This-- this was unheard of. Never had been ever-- no one had ever been deported by the British before. OK, we came-- we came as illegals, but even so. You know?

INT: So they offered passive resistance.

HW: Passive resistance. So what they-- what-- so what they did was that the camp was encircled by a British military brigade. They set up machine guns all over the place, and then the police-- then British police arrived. The military didn't involve themselves. The British-- Pale-- the sort of Palestine police, they came about 30 or 40 people per barrack. There were about-- it was 30 or 40 people-- 30 people per barrack.

So the police came with overwhelming force. They decided to clean out barrack by barrack. So they came, they cleaned out on barrack. If-- if people were not dressed, they took them, wrapped them in a blanket, and threw them on the truck. Once they had 10 or 15 people on a truck, the truck took off. OK?

That-- that's the way they operated. In this way, they were able to-- to clear off-- it took about-- took about, I don't know, five, six hours, to-- to--

INT: How were you treated by the British?

HW: Well, that was a very specific incident. You know? Then, [LAUGHS] believe it or not, we had to go through a customs examination. But this is really unheard of. You know, what happened was this so-called passive resistance collapsed after about a few hours.

This was also done only by the younger people. When they came to our hut, which was near the end, the passive resistance had already collapsed, and my father and I, we were dressed. So when we're dressed, we're put on a truck, and going to the port.

At the port, we had to go through a customs examination. I mean, this is really incredible. So-- so I had to-- I had [INAUDIBLE] my luggage. He says, you come to customs. Open your bag, please. [LAUGHS] I had to open that. He says, what's this? I had a gel for hair cream or what that. He says, that-- somebody could-- something could be in the hair cream. He took that away.

A lot of stuff got stolen, you know, by the way. Some cameras and valuable things were stolen, especially of those people who left completely naked, because they left everything behind. It was all gathered up by some-- some Arab prisoners whom the British had assembled for this purpose. All the stuff was gathered up in just heaps and sent off to-- to the ship.

And there were two ships there, the [? Johann David ?] and the New Zealand. They were two Dutch ships whom the Brit-- whom the British had confiscated. And those two ships sailed for Mauritius within a day or so. My father and I, we were on one ship. I was on the [? Johann David, ?] I think, my father and I, and my mother was on the other ship.

Because-- my father and I we were in Acre, and my mother was in Atlit. They were different camps, so we got on two different ships. But the ships sailed-- the ships left at the same time and sailed for Mauritius. It took about two weeks to get there.

It was a long trip, because you have to go through-- through the Swiss canal, then all through the Red Sea, right? Then through the Indian Ocean, and all zig-zag because of the U-boat. The ships went zigzagging. So it takes-- therefore it takes much longer unless you-- you don't go straight.

It took about-- I don't know. We arrived at [INAUDIBLE]. We left the-- the-- the ships left-- left-- the ships left on December the 9th, and arrived in Mauritius on December the 26th.

INT: Did you encounter the war on that trip?

HW: No. No. We did not encounter a U-boat or anything else. No.

INT: And what were the conditions on that ship like?

HW: Oh, it was pretty good. We had-- [LAUGHS] except we had-- we had la-- we had mutton every day. After-- after-- after three or four days I couldn't eat mutton anymore. We had mutton for twice a day. Not for breakfast, but for lunch and for dinner.

Afterward, we went on strike. We couldn't eat mutton anymore. They must have had a tremendous amount of mutton on board, frozen, you know? I couldn't eat it anymore. [LAUGHS]

INT: Did you know at that point where they were taking you?

HW: I-- I don't remember. I think we found out that it's going to Mauritius, but I don't know.

INT: What happened to the people on the boat? How-- what was the morale like? What were they feeling?

HW: Low. It was-- everybody was very depressed. You know, what are we going to do? What-- what-- Mauritius? Who has ever heard of a thing of this place? I mean, what do people know about Mauritius? I knew about Mauritius only one thing, there's a famous stamp. The blau-- the blau-- the blue Mauritius stamp.

And it's a tiny island somewhere in the Indian Ocean. What are we going to do there? What's-- you know? Although we were saved from Germany. OK, that's fine. Great. But, I mean, what are we going to do on this tropical island far away from anywhere? And who knows how we-- how long are we going to stay there and all that? It was very-- it was a very depressing thing.

INT: What happened once you got there?

HW: Well, once we-- we-- we got there and we, of course, immediately unloaded onto buses or whatever. And we were brought to our camp. Now, our camp was actually in a place called [? Bobasan ?].

Let me say, Mauritius is a beautiful, tropical island with vegetation like you've never seen. There is green and wild flowers to very steep mountains, and they have green right to the top. It looks like a tropical paradise. Absolutely. These wild flowers and flowering trees you've never seen. It's absolutely magnificent.

It has become, now, I understand, a big holiday resort. People fly there from Paris. They have regular flights from Paris to Mauritius. People fly there, you know? They have their-- these camps. You know?

But at that time, it didn't exist. At that time, there was only-- you could only go there by boat. So we arrived at our camp. The camp was really not a camp. It was a prison. What the-- what the British had done, they had cleared the prison of the prisoners, and we were sent to this prison.

Now, the prison was organized with two huge cell blocks. Each cell blocks had about 300 or 400 cells-- each individual cells, and a wall around. And there were several other buildings in there, like workshops and storage and so forth. But they also had built a-- another camp adjacent to it for-- with barracks for about 30-- 30 persons each.

And the way it was organized so all the men were in the prison with the cells, while the women were in the adjacent camp. In other words, each man had a cell to himself, while the women were about 20 or 25 women to one barrack. And that's the way, actually, it stayed for-- for the whole time, for the rest of the period we were in that camp.

INT: Were you under guard there, or were you--

HW: Yes, of course. We were never allowed to go. We were under guard by the local police. But the-- the-- the sergeant and the-- and the upper-- upper echelon, so to speak, were all British. So the-- the camp commandant [INAUDIBLE] the certain captain armitage was a British police captain.

And there were several police sergeants that had accompanied us from Palestine and who stayed there for four years, actually. There were two police-- two or three police sergeants from the Palestine police-- British Palestine police-- who stayed with us in Mauritius.

INT: How were you treated there? Were you treated like prisoners?

HW: No. No. We were treated like internees. Let's say it this way. We had complete freedom inside the camp. You know? Nobody bothered us inside the camp. We were not allowed to go out. We had-- you know, we could send as many letters as we wished and-- and so forth. We had our own kitchens and our own cultural activities.

I can't go into this a little bit more in greater detail, of course. Right? It took some time to get-- get organized. So we arrived first, it was-- first of all, in the first instance everybody was very depressing because what was the future going to be? We are in this camp on this-- on this godforsaken island.

There are no Jews, by the way. Not one Jew living in Mauritius. Not one. Most of the population is black-- is-- is Creole. The-- the-- the language of the country is a kind of a Creole French, because at one time, Mauritius was French until Napoleon lost the war, and then it became a British colony.

So, at that time-- at that-- and now it's an independent state, but at the time it was a British colony. And there were also many Indians living there. The main-- the main industry in Mauritius-- even today, I think-- is the sugar. Sugar in the cane-- sugar cane, which is being-- being cut down and-- and refined and then sent to England, most the time.

But we were-- we were cut off from the local population. We were living in this-- in this camp for-- for four years, and had very little contact with-- with the local population because we were not allowed to go out. Except once in a while, we got a day pass to go in to town for a day. But we had to go back-- we had to be back in in the evening. And once in a while we were allowed to go to a beach to go swimming, under escort-- under police escort.

INT: Can I stop you there for a moment?

HW: Sure.

INT: Thank you.

HW: [LAUGHS]INT: Tell us about life on Mauritius.

HW: Well, after a while, we got organized. Many people-- first of all, I must say, in the beginning we had a-- a terrible typhus epidemic. Because I guess it was a reaction of what had happened, you know, the trip and the disappointment of being sent there.

And I guess we also didn't get mosquito nets in time. So as a result, many people got malaria. Because for about a week or so, we didn't have mosquito nets.

And that is an absolute necessity, because there are millions of these things around. And I guess as a reaction of the-- of the lack of [SIGHS] cleanliness on-- on board of the ship, the typhus epidemic broke out in a delayed kind of-- as a delayed action. Actually, some people had died already on the ship, but nobody knew about it. Anyway, the fact is, it broke out in Mauritius. And within about three weeks 40 people died, and that's it.

INT: Did you get sick?

HW: No, my mother, only my mother. I was not sick. I eventually got malaria. Eventually, after a few years, I had malaria. My father never had it, and my mother had malaria and typhus at the same time in the very beginning. And then she didn't-- she survived, somehow. She-- she was very ill, but she survived.

My father, who never got anything-- he's very-- was a very tough, tough man. And I didn't get anything, except I think I got eventually malaria. But it wasn't too bad. I went to the hospital a few times, because it was very nasty. And it had a few nasty attacks. But it wasn't-- wasn't-- too bad.

Well, life in-- life in general started to settle down. But let me explain to you, first of all, how life was. There was the situation where the men were in the men's camp, the women were in the women's camp. No, there were many, many, married couples with children and so forth.

Now, how was-- first of all, how was it going to be organized that the men and women got together? Now, in the-- in the very beginning, for the first few months, it was-- the-- the regulation was that in the afternoon every day, for a certain number of hours, I think in the afternoon-- that changed from time to time-- men and women could get together in an open area adjacent to the camp. OK. But it was an open area, like a-- like a garden.

You know, like a vegetable garden. But you could sit there on the grass and chat. And then there was a-- then the police whistled and the men had to go back to the men's camp, women to the women's camp. But eventually, especially the married people, complained to the-- to the-- to the management-- not to the management, to the commandant-- that said, after all, look, listen, what-- what about the sex life? You see what I mean?

But-- because what happened was that after a while, during these afternoons, people used to put up tents. And all kinds of things were going on in these tents. And the British are very prude-- prude-- prudish, prudish yeah? Prudish.

And they didn't like what was going on in these tents. It's not very nice, and so on and so forth. So our-- our-- our leaders said, look, it's your fault. Something has to be done.

So various things were proposed, a camp for married people only and another camp for unmarried men and the third camp for unmarried women. This was rejected, because we had now two camps. Now, we had to go to three camps and people are even more separated.

Then eventually, what came-- what was-- was finally agreed on between our-- our people and-- and the camp commandant was as follows. Since the men's camp was a prison where every man had a cell-- which was, you could close the door. The cell doors were not locked, by the way. You couldn't leave the camp. But within the camp, you were free to do whatever you want, all right?

So, actually, every man had their privacy, right? So what was done was that those women who were married and who had a husband in the camp, they got a passport for only certain hours during the day. But never during the night, because it is indecent to stay with your husband during the night. It's indecent. You can only do that during the day-- during daylight hours.

So during the day, they were allowed to visit the men's camp and visit-- they had to go back-- these-- these times changed from time to time. They were eventually extended I think until about 10:00 in the evening, but never more than that and never overnight. For the rest of the period, as far as I know, it was never allowed-- it was never permitted for women to stay overnight in the men's camp.

Now, what happened [LAUGHS] once was we had a radio in the camp. And the radio was tuned into the BBC from London. And, of course, since the language in the camp was mostly German, we were only allowed to-- to-- to listen to the German language broadcasts of the BBC London.

'Here, East London,' [SINGING] bah, bah, bah, bah bah bah. That was every day. There was a news bulletin at 8 o'clock, and at 10 o'clock, and so forth.

And so we had-- there were loud speakers at certain parts in the camp. But there was one central radio room where there was the radio. And there was also a gramophone where we had some records, where we could play some records for entertainment.

And there was at one time, a ship was sunk by a U-boat in the Indian Ocean. And there was a rumor amongst the native population that we have a radio transmitter in the camp and we had been in touch with enemy U-boats. And because we are German speaking-- these people didn't know about Jews and stuff-- we are German agents, you know, who are in touch with the Germans, and we have a radio in the camp. But, of course, we had only a receiver, not the transmitter, you understand?

So-- so-- but the British had to do something about this rumor. So what they did-- they-- there was-- there were never any roll reports, because you couldn't escape there anyway, right? Even if you were-- even if you went over the wall and into the-- to outside, you can't escape from that island. Once in a month a ship comes, and so forth. It's impossible.

Anyway, they did a roll call one fine day, in the middle of the night. In the middle of the night, the-- the British police came. And there was a general roll call.

And they found a few women in the men's camp who had stayed with their husbands illegally. So they were punished. I think their passport was withdrawn for about a month, or something like this. So that was the only time that women were allowed-- stayed overnight.

But life became more normal. How should I say normal? We had a school. A school was established for school children. We had a number of intelligent people who spoke many languages.

And there were professors, and doctors, and Jewish middle class people, all kinds of people who were giving courses. I start-- took Hebrew courses and English. Of course, I studied English, because that's where I basically started to learn English in the camp. I start to-- we were eventually [? able-- ?] able to establish a library, a big library, quite a big library.

We got a-- books were sent to us from South Africa. The Jewish community from South Africa was the closest one, so they sent us a whole library and some money, also. Donations were made.

But nobody was ever allowed to visit us. They tried to visit. It was never allowed to-- nobody was ever-- from the Jewish agency or Zionist organizations was not allowed to visit the camp. There was even a case where somebody-- somebody's son, who was a-- on the-- in the Merchant Navy, or something, he was in Mauritius.

And he want to visit his mother. He was only allowed to visit his mother in the front office. He was-- she was brought to the front office. And he was brought in, so he was able to visit his mother there. And then he had to leave again and that it.

So it was very strict. And also, the British said we are never allowed to go back to Palestine. That was it, since we had broken the regulations. After the war, we would be sent back where we came from. Because the war-- they wanted to win the war-- the British, right?

And we will go back to Austria, and to Germany, and to Poland, wherever we came from-- Czechoslovakia-- and that was it. So in the meantime, of course, we were not cut off from the whole world we-- because we-- newspapers were sent to us, you see, from Palestine, from England, from South Africa. So we knew what was going on.

We knew all about the Holocaust. Because we got the newspapers from Palestine. And we got the JewishChroniclefrom London and from other papers. So we knew everything that was going on.

We knew. And on top of it, as you-- as you-- I've have showed you before, we got even some Red Cross letters from enemy territories. That took a long time to reach Mauritius. It took about six or eight months.

Even an ordinary letter from London, England, or from-- from Palestine, to go to Mauritius took about two, three months. A letter, because there was no air mail. It all had to go by ordinary boat and to go through the censorship and all of that.

So it took a long time. So in other words, we were in a remote place, which was the thing that bothers us most. Because we're in this remote place cut off from-- from everywhere.

But we tried to-- we tried to entertain ourselves. We had schools. We had our own football team. You name it, we got it. We had coffee houses.

We had theater groups. We had several theater groups. We had a-- we had a music-- we had a music band called The [INAUDIBLE] Boys, The [INAUDIBLE]. There was a big scandal at one time. One of the [INAUDIBLE] Boys got one of the-- got one-- a British girl from high society, got her pregnant.

And he had to leave the-- he had to leave the band as a result of that. He was a very good looking fellow. And they fell in love, and she got pregnant. It was a terrible scandal. Terrible scandal, I'll tell you. And he had to leave the band. [LAUGHS]

INT: How long were you on Mauritius?

HW: I was-- I left Mauritius in March of 1945.

INT: And why did you leave?

HW: Well, what happened was that during the war, the Zionist authorities approached or appealed to the British to allow a Jewish division to be formed-- to be formed to fight against the Nazis. Well, the-- the British didn't want to do that for political reasons. Because they figured-- I don't know. Didn't want.

But eventually, Mr. Churchill, I think, was persuaded to-- to-- to agree to that. And eventually, I think in 1944 or so-- I'm not sure about the date-- the Jewish Brigade Group was formed. Now, there were 30,000 Jews had-- had enlisted from Palestine.

And they were spread over many units, all kinds of small Jewish units. A company here, and a-- and a battalion here, but they were all in different formations. Many were in the Eighth Army that were fighting in North Africa against Rommel.

INT: And this was all in the British Army?

HW: That was all part of the British army. Oh yes, yes, yes. And then eventually, one fine day, a telegram arrived from the Jewish agency that volunteers from the camp are allowed to join the Jewish brigade group.

It was a great honor. Because, for instance, Jews from England were not allowed, or from America, or from-- from anywhere else were not allowed to join them the Jewish Brigade Group, only from Palestine and from our camp. Oh, by the way, in the meantime, since we had a number of Czech citizens and Polish citizens, they were allowed-- about 80 Czech citizens were allowed to join the Czech Army. And about 40 or 50 Polish citizens were allowed to join the Polish army.

And they left Mauritius in-- in several groups. But since-- since, for instance, I was an Austrian citizen. Like an enemy, alien, I wasn't allowed to-- to join the British Army. Except, a few left also to join the British Pioneer Corps. There were about 20 or 30 who joined the British Pioneer Corps, Austrians, Jewish. Everyone was Jewish in the camp practically.

But I didn't want to join the-- the Pioneer Corps as an Austrian. But when-- when this thing came in at the end of 1944, I volunteered together with about 40 or 50 other people. And eventually, I signed. And I found the thing here, right?

I found the document here. I signed, officially, the declaration on February the 1, 1945. By that time, it was already [LAUGHS] very late in the war. And I left shortly thereafter. And, of course, the rest of the-- my parents, stayed in the camp.

I left together with these other 40 people on a military transport, Mauritius for East Africa. We went from there to Kenya. And in Kenya, we stayed for about two weeks, and then we left from there with another ship for the Middle East.

Now, that was now in the spring of 1945. The Jewish Brigade Group at that time was in Italy, was fighting in Italy as part of the Eighth Army somewhere in Italy north of Rome, somewhere near Bologna. I don't know exactly.

But, of course, we had to go to-- to Egypt. Near the Suez Canal zone, there was the-- the Jewish Brigade Group [? that ?] depot and training center. So we arrived there in the Middle East in the- in the Suez Canal zone somewhere in March. And we went into-- into-- into basic-- what is called basic training. And we went through that basic training, which takes about two or three months.

Now, by that time this was already in May of 1945. And on May 8 of 1945-- I think, May 8, I think it was-- there war came to an end. Germany surrendered. OK. Shortly thereafter, the British government decided that the people in Mauritius were allowed to go after all, if they wanted to, to go to Palestine.

In other words, anyone who was in the camp of Mauritians had a choice. They had a choice. Either they could go back to-- go to Palestine. Or, if they wanted, they could go back to the country where they came from.

In other words, some people decided to go back to Vienna. There was, for instance, in Mauritius, a group of about 30 or 40 Jewish communists. These people were more communist than Jews.

They had never known, even, that they were Jews. But under the Hitler laws, they-- they-- suddenly discovered themselves as Jews. But they were-- they were communists.

And they wanted to go back to Vienna. And they did. They want to build a communist state in Austria. So they decided to go back to Vienna.

And a few, a few, about 30 or 40, went back to Czechoslovakia. And even one or two went back to Germany. I know one back-- one back-- went back to Germany, one or two.

But the great majority, including my parents, didn't want to go back. About 95% of the people in Mauritius decided to go-- wanted to go to Palestine, including my parents. So in May-- I think, in May-- they all packed up their-- their things and went back to Palestine.

In the meantime, I was in the Middle East in the Suez Canal zone doing the basic training. But my parents went back to Palestine. That was in May or the beginning of June of 1945. So my parents went to Palestine, and that was very nice. They got one month's leave.

In other words, there was-- an appeal was made to the Jewish population. Anybody who has not got any relatives could be-- could stay with a family-- with a Jewish family in Palestine. My parents stayed with a Jewish family in a place called-- called Pardes Hanna.

Pardes Hanna, you know the place? OK. My parents got a little house there. They had a house.

They had an orange grove, these people. And they had a little house there. And my parents got a room in that house. And there they stayed for about a month.

And I was allowed to visit them there. So we got a one week vacation, because they-- they knew that they were in Palestine. So I got there and met my parents. And then after that, my parents got an apartment near Haifa.

Located by the-- not a very nice-- a not very-- it was a one-room apartment. One room, a small-- a small room-- it was much smaller than this, let me tell you. It was about this-- this size, [LAUGHS] this size and-- and a very tiny kitchen and a bathroom that had to be shared with somebody else. It was in the middle of nowhere in a place called Kiryat Haim. Maybe you know the place. It's near Haifa in the Kiryat just outside Haifa.

INT: Tell me, Mr. Wellisch, how you felt when you met your parents in Palestine.

HW: Well, I felt fine. You know? I mean, [LAUGHTER] I hadn't-- you know, I had left them only a few months before. You know I mean? So--

INT: Yeah, but it was different situation.

HW: It was very nice, you know, under the circumstances of course. And I was glad that they-- that they had been said to receive such a wonderful reception, you know. After all, you know, after all these-- these years, they were taken care of, you know? It was very nice that they had this family who-- I was-- yeah, I was very touched, you know.

INT: So what did you do after that?

HW: Yes.

INT: Did you continue with the Jewish Brigade?

HW: Yes. Well, what happened was-- so by-- by-- by May of 19-- 1945, the war was over. The Jewish Brigade Group-- when the war was finished, the Jewish Brigade was still in northern Italy when the war-- when Germany surrendered. Now, it was part of the Jewish-- of the British Army. Now, the British Army at that time occupied part of Germany, right? But for political reasons, the British did not want to send the Jewish Brigade Group as an occupation force to Germany.

Oh, by the way, the Jewish Brigade Group danced the hora in Rome around the Titus-- around the Titus arch. Somebody told me. I wasn't there, but somebody told me that. [LAUGHS] You know, as a little thing.

The Jewish Brigade Group was about 6,000 or 7,000 people. It's like a small division with-- with artillery and so forth and so on. I was eventually allocated to the-- to the engineering corps. The Jewish Brigade was stationed in Holland and Belgium.

First, I went to Antwerp. I still have a passport, which I was there in Antwerp. It was a few months. Then I was transferred to another unit, all within the Jewish Brigade Group, to Eindhoven.

And then finally, I was sent to Breda, which is also in Holland, a small town in Holland. And then somebody said to me-- you know, it was very boring. It was so boring. You cannot imagine how boring it was-- had nothing to do.

Because what you do there in army, in peace time, is the most boring thing that you can imagine. The only thing that we did was we tried to smuggle refugees to Palestine. That was our main function at that time. That was the only thing we really did.

And we had all the-- we-- we faked papers. And we supplied people with fake papers and fake uniforms, and sent them as fake Jewish soldiers on leave to Palestine and all kinds of other things. Sometimes they were caught, then they were sent back. Then they were sent again. So there was a continuous traffic.

INT: How long did you continue on in the Jewish Brigade Group?

HW: Well, what happened was, on fine day somebody said to me, you know, what are you doing here anyway? Your parents are elderly people. You really have to look after your parents. You should ask for a home posting.

I said, what do you mean home posting? I'm here with the Jewish Brigade. He says, no, you're actually part of the British Army. You could ask. There is such a thing as a home posting, because you want to be near your parents. You have to look after parents, especially an only child.

And he says, I-- I tell you, you go to this particular place, you fill in all these forms, and you put it in. See what happens. I did. And like magic, within two weeks [LAUGHS] I was transferred back to Palestine. Now, in Palestine there was a very serious situation.

In Palestine, in 1946, there was this-- the [NON-ENGLISH]. They were-- they were-- they were conducting a terrorist campaign against the British. They were blowing up bridges and so forth. There was a lot of bad feeling between the British Army and the Jewish population of Palestine.

Because-- because these underground groups-- the Stern Gang, and the Stern Group or whatever you call them, and the [NON-ENGLISH] were conducting this-- this campaign. Now, I was-- I was sent there as a Jewish soldier as being part of the British-- British Army. So they didn't-- I arrived there in this British camp in [INAUDIBLE], which was a central British depot.

Just-- I arrived. Just then, there was an attack on the camp by the terrorists, a terrorist attack. Sirens were going. It was shooting and so forth. And they looked at me as if I were a spy. You see what I mean?

So anyway-- but they had to-- but they were fair, nothing anti-Semitic or anything. They were very fair. They said, look, where do your parents live? I said, my parents are living in Kiryat Haim, near Haifa.

Well, they looked up the list. You are in the Royal Army Service Corps. Well, he says, yes, we have a place for you. There is a field bakery not far from Kiryat Haim.

And we'll post you to the field bakery. And you'll be-- but I said, I'm not a field baker. I'm not a baker. I'm-- I'm-- I'm nothing. You know, I'm-- I'm not-- But my ranking in the Royal Army Service Corps-- your rank is not-- my rank was-- was driver.

Now, I'm not a-- at that time, I didn't know how to drive a car. But a rank is driver. Like private in the Army, in the Royal Army Service Corps, you're a driver. You can still see that on my-- on some of my papers. [LAUGHS]

So they sent me to this field bakery. So they arrive at the field bakery, which is in the-- in the [INAUDIBLE]. You know where the oil refinery is? It was not far from the oil refinery, about 15 minutes by bus from where my parents were, in Kiryat Haim. Which was very nice.

So I arrived at the bakery. Ah, the driver has arrived. But I said, I'm not a driver. [LAUGHS] I said, it's my rank. Well, he said, what are you doing here then? We don't-- we need a driver.

We don't need a rank-- we need a driver driver. Because we drive the truck. But I said, I'm not a driver. Well, what shall we do with you? I said, [LAUGHS] that's not-- that's not my business. It's your business.

OK, so they didn't know what to do with me, so I didn't do anything. So they gave me a pass. But they-- but since I'm not a baker, they couldn't send me to the bakery. Because they are not a baker.

And since I'm not a driver-- they didn't want to give me a rifle either to do guard duty, because the camp has to be guarded. Yeah. Every military-- they didn't want to do that either, because of the political situation with the Jewish attacks on these camps. So they-- so I didn't do anything.

So what I did was I got a permanent pass that I slept at my parents' home. In the morning, I arrived at the camp, had breakfast. Then I went into the canteen, and I read a book. Then I had lunch.

Then after lunch, I-- I was sitting in the afternoon until about 4:00 or 5:00, 4 'o'clock, then I had an early supper. And then I left again. And then I did-- and that I did for several months until I was discharged in, I think, September of 1946.

I was discharged, I went back to [INAUDIBLE], I got a complete discharge out-- a suit, tie-- tie, a shirt-- everything, a pair of shoes and everything, a hat and everything. And I was discharged from the British Army in, I think, September or October, 19-- 1946. And that was my service with the-- with the British Army.

So then I joined my parents. Now, in the meantime, my father had got a job with the-- in the British Army camp as a-- as a stock keeper. He didn't speak one word of English, but he still got the job as a stock. Because somebody else got him the job.

He-- what he did there, I have no idea. But he got a job, which was paid Which it paid very little, but it's better than nothing.

My father was a businessman. He couldn't find anything else to do. He didn't speak Hebrew. He spoke only German and Hungarian, of course. It's very important, you know. What you're going to do in Israel with speaking in German and Hungarian?

Anyway, so I arrived there and lived with my parents. We were all living in one room, which was smaller-- I would say about this size. The room was so small that we had a pull-out bed. We had one bed. And under this bed, you could pull out the other two beds.

Because, otherwise, you couldn't move around. And our table, the table that we used was a folding table. Because, otherwise, there was not enough table-- not enough space. Only when we had lunch, we used to unfold the table, so we had lunch, [LAUGHS] you know? Then we had to fold it.

Because we had a-- we had a wardrobe. Over there, there are no built-in wardrobes. You have to have wardrobe with the three-- where we had our things. [LAUGHS] You know, it's-- it's really funny.

INT: How long did you live in Palestine?

HW: Till, I think, '51. [LAUGHS]

INT: Were you in the Israeli Army?

HW: Yeah. Now, let me say, let me say-- after, I had to do some work, right? I had to make a living. So in the camp in Mauritius-- I hadn't-- I didn't go into that, OK? I think I told you that I was-- in Vienna, I started out as a-- as a cabinet maker apprentice.

So in the camp in Mauritius, there was a cabinet-- a carpenter or cabinet making shop. You had to do something. Otherwise, you go mad. So everybody did-- did something.

So I went to-- I got a -- a job. I worked at the-- at the cabinet making shop doing work for work within the camp let's say, repairing beds and things like that. So about over four years, I built up a certain knowledge of cabinet making and carpentry.

So when I arrived in-- in Palestine, I had a quite a certain knowledge of this trade. Not that great, but even so I knew quite a bit. And I had a cousin there on my mother's side who was an-- an architect interior decorator. And he knew a number of cabinet makers. And he got me a job with one of these cabinet makers.

And I did some work. And there I learned, very quickly, some more in this field. And we did-- mostly, we did custom-made furniture. That's what we did, very fine furniture, fine veneered furniture. We get our own veneering.

And, you know, custom made furniture-- let's say somebody wants to have a dining room set. We did them a dining room set. It was designed specifically for this particular person, you know, the size and everything, or a bedroom set, or whatever, a built-in cupboard and so forth.

That's what-- that's what we did. And eventually, this man gave up the business. And we took over the-- the employees took over the firm as a co-operative. So there were about four or five people in the co-operative.

INT: Can I stop you there please?

HW: Sure.INT: Working as a carpenter, and the political situation in Israel was--

HW: It was deteriorating.

INT: It was deteriorating.

HW: Because remember, I got my discharge in 1946. And '46, '47, there was really, the United Nations decided to do a partition. And eventually, in May of 1948, the-- the-- the state of Israel was born on May the 15th, which is another day I will never forget, of course.

You know, in the meantime, I was a discharged soldier, right? All discharged soldiers joined the Haganah, which was the underground Israeli army. But in the meantime, all I did at that time, during those up-- those times when there were these-- were these unrest, we did just local guard duty in our area.

We had four rifles for our area, but we were not allowed-- we had four rifles. I think we had four rounds per rifle, and we were not allowed to use these-- we were not allowed to fire the rifle. What is the purpose of a rifle if you're not allowed to fire it, I don't know. Because if you fired the rifle, you'll hear it, and then the British police would come and take it away.

But anyway, and I was deferred. You see, I was called up to the-- to the-- to the full-time duty at the beginning of '48-- yeah, in February or March. But since I was an only son, [NON-ENGLISH]. There was-- there was a regulation at the time, [NON-ENGLISH] are deferred. OK? So I was deferred.

But on 15th of May, all deferments were canceled because there was an-- there was an emergency situation, because the Arab states were invading from all sides. So on the-- on that particular day, there-- there was a sign-- there were-- there were placards all over the place, all these deferred. And I had to report to a certain place. And I left together with a friend of mine, for Tel Aviv, on a bus.

I arrived in Tel Aviv. From Tel Aviv, we were sent to a place called Tel Litwinsky, which was a former huge British army camp. And there, we were standing all in one long line. And on the same day, there was an air attack on the-- on the-- on the camp, and there were two Egyptian spitfires, they were-- they were machine gunning our camp. And I was watching those machine-- machine gunning our camp. Those are very interesting. But I-- I wasn't afraid somehow.

Anyway, they were machine gunning our camp, and they left. And then there was an officer going along the line. It was an officer because he had the sign which said [NON-ENGLISH]. It didn't have any insignia. At that time, there were no insignia.

You had to add an armband, it said [NON-ENGLISH], or whatever or [NON-ENGLISH]. [NON-ENGLISH] means officer, [NON-ENGLISH] means sergeant. So this one was [NON-ENGLISH]. And he was from South Africa. He was-- he was a regular soldier, apparently.

And he spoke with everybody, and he-- there was a-- my-- my friend next to me was a very tall, good looking fellow. He says, he says to him, 'you been in the army before?' He says, yes. 'What army?' He was Jewish-- he was also in the Jewish brigade group. He says, Jewish brigade. Ah, he says. Step aside.

So he stepped aside, and all these people-- he took about 30 people. They-- they all left. And-- and my friend was-- two weeks later, he was-- he was killed in the Battle of Latrun, if you-- if you heard about that. That was an unfortunate battle where many people were killed.

Then-- then another one came and said, 'everybody who is an electrician, carpenter, automotive mechanic, and this, and step forward. So since I was a carpenter, I stepped forward.

So, like, OK, he says. 'All come with us.' So we were about 30 or 40 people. We all went on attack, and we left.

And we joined-- and we joined-- at this particular time, we joined the engineering corps. Now, what was the engineering corps? As I found out, I was not that happy about it.

The engineering corps was mostly laying and dismantling of mines, which is a very dangerous and nasty job. And everybody was really-- we were not very happy that we had volunteered for this particular job, you know what I mean?

But after-- we-- we-- we took this course at an engineering school. And after a while, we got used to that, you know? If you know what you're doing, and you do what-- you do exactly things according to the book, then there's very little chance of anything will happen to you. You do exactly the way it's supposed to be done, and you're going to be all right. OK?

And that's what we did. I joined the engineering corps, and eventually I-- I became part of a platoon, of an engineering platoon. And we started to lay mines left and right and start dismantling mines. And nothing ever happened except once, because somebody did something stupid he was not supposed to do. You know, those mines, there are-- there are anti-personnel mines, and there are anti-tank mines.

The anti-tank mines, if a tank or a-- a truck goes over it, it blows up. But if a person stands-- steps on it, nothing happens because it needs a heavy weight. Now, the anti-personnel mines are different.

They have a-- the mine is buried underground, and then there's a thin wire. And if you trip the wire, the mine jumps up. Our mines were the Israeli made mines. They were very good. It's a mine that jumps up about that high.

It doesn't explode then. It only jumps up about that high. Because there are two explosions. There's one explosion which throws the mine up, and then there's a second explosion at about this height, when the mine exploded. And it-- it-- it kills everything within a certain radius.

So what happened was we laid a minefield, and we were going home, and one fellow had a stick in his hand. And he took the stick, and he threw it. And of course, the stick landed on the wire. The wire set off the mine. It jumped up.

[MAKES SOUND]

And one was injured. He wasn't even killed, he was injured. Because you're not supposed to throw a stick, you see what I mean? So anyway--

INT: How long were you in the Israeli army?

HW: About eight or nine months.

INT: And after you were in the army, did you-- what did you do then? Did you--

HW: Well, I returned again to my cabinet making. Then I-- I had this one job, and then this fairly-- this place kind of folded. And then I got a job with another cabinet maker in Haifa. We had to travel every day to Haifa. And I had-- I had this job with this-- with this man in-- with this cabinet maker in Haifa until about '51.

In the meantime, of course, my father, after '48, he lost his job with the British army camp, and he was very unhappy. He was very unhappy. He really deteriorated. He was sitting at home all the time. And then my father's sister, who had been in England and these people had emigrated to Canada, to Montreal. And they established a factory in Canada, in Montreal, making light fixtures.

And my father's sister and the husband and the son, it was all a fa-- all a big family. They said, 'what-- what are you doing there? Why don't you come to Canada?'

My father's going to get a job, and as far as I'm concerned, there's no problem. You know, I will get a job. I have a good profession. 'Why don't you come to Canada? You-- you're going to get a job in the factory, and you'll be OK.'

I didn't-- I wasn't that-- I wasn't that enthusiastic. Of course, I was certain a Zionist, you know? I had become a Zionist over the years. But I was somehow in the depressed mood.

Many of my friends had been killed in the war. So my best friend had been killed immediately at Latrun, and several others of my friends had been killed. And I was there, I felt kind of-- I don't know.

I-- I was doing the [INAUDIBLE] job. I'd preferred to have-- I would have preferred to go to a kibbutz. This I would-- but I couldn't do that either. I couldn't leave my parents, you know, alone in the city, and I go away to a kibbutz. You know, it's not possible. I had a cousin in a kibbutz, so I could have joined him, but I-- I didn't-- I couldn't do that. You know, I couldn't leave my parents.

So eventually-- eventually, my parents pushed and pushed, you know, pushed me. And eventually agreed to that we were going to leave Palestine. So we left.

We got the-- my-- my uncle and his son and all that, they-- they-- they guaranteed, you see. They said my father is going to get a job. And I was younger, there was no question for me.

They guaranteed anyway, and we got the-- we got the-- we would have-- see, there was no Canadian consulate in Israel. OK? There was no Canadian consulate. The clear-- the nearest consulate was one in Rome, and there was one in London. So we decided to got to London because my uncle had some friends there, and they would look after us while we were staying in London.

So we-- so we packed our bags, we gave up the-- gave up the house, the apartment, and we left. We sold whatever we had to sell. We had a very nice radio, which we sold. [CHUCKLES] And I said goodbye to all my friends, and I left-- I left Israel, which--

I remember when I left Israel, at the time when we were leaving, there was a big immigration flow from Iraq. And I remember when we were leaving, there were all these people coming in from Iraq. And I remember seeing all these people getting off the plane and kissing the ground. See, I saw that.

Coming from Iraq, these-- these Jewish people come from Iraq, kissing the ground as they got off the plane. Yeah. It was 1951. I think at that time, most-- most of the Iraqi Jews left.

So we went to England, to London, to have another little adventure. My-- my uncle's friend had booked a ship for us to go to Canada. At that time, thousands of people were going to Canada.

Everything was sold out, but we had the reservation on-- on a ship. All planes were all sold out. They were Trans-Canada Airlines at that time. There was no Air Canada.

So my father went to-- my father had never been sick in his life. My mother also not. The mother-- anyway, we went to the-- to the Canadian Medical, just like nothing. The medical doctor, I think, what's very anti-Semitic, I think. He was a French Canadian, I think he was. He looked at us as if we-- as if we were dirt, you know? He-- I can still remember that.

Anyway, but we went to the medical, and then we didn't get an answer. We had a transit-- a British transit visa only for one month. And we had booked a certain date on the ship for about-- we had about-- about three weeks between arrival in England and leaving England, do you see what I mean? And we were staying at a [INAUDIBLE] in London. And we didn't get anything from the British consulate.

So I went there and said, 'listen, what's-- our ship is going to go in about a week. What's the matter? Is there any problem? So eventually, I found out there was a problem. Apparently, they had found something on my father's lung, that he had something, some-- but he had never been sick.

Anyway, they nearly didn't want him to go to Canada. They said he has-- they can't decide it here, and he would have to go to-- he would-- they would have to send these things to Ottowa, that the Minister of Health would have to decide. And eventually, eventually, they-- they changed their minds, and we got-- we got the visa.

By that time, our-- our reservation on the ship had run out. So we got, eventually, another one. We have to get one first class, so we had to suffer and go first class on the Ascania, which I still-- I still have the first class, the goodbye dinner, the first class. And the man said, 'you would like a steak?' We says, OK. He says, 'you would like to have another steak?' I say, 'yes, sure.'

I had never eaten a steak in my life. You know, after living in Mauritius for five years in Palestine. And in Israel, there was very little food. We got about one little piece of fish once a week because there was very-- in '51 and '50, there was very little to eat in Israel-- in Israel. It was terrible.

And before, we had lived in-- in Mauritius, so I hadn't-- I hadn't eaten a steak in 10 years. I hadn't eaten butter for about 10 years, you know what I mean? This was-- only margarine and stuff like this.

INT: When did you arrive in Canada?

HW: I have to check. I think in July of 1951.

INT: And you arrived where?

HW: In Montreal.

INT: And did you live in Montreal?

HW: Yeah, that's true. Because my uncle, he had the factory there, and we lived in Montreal. Very close, on-- on the-- you know-- you know Montreal a little bit? On [INAUDIBLE], not far from the Jewish-- Jewish general hospital. In the-- in the [INAUDIBLE] district.

INT: And did you get married there in Montreal?

HW: Yeah, yeah, I got-- I got married there. I arrived there in '51. I was married in '62. My parents died shortly after I got married. My father died in '63 and my mother in '64.

INT: And do you have any children?

HW: Yeah. And in '65, I-- I-- my daughter was born. Unfortunately, my parents never-- never made it to see my daughter. So my daughter was born in-- in '65.

And we continued living in Montreal until '69-- '68-- '69. And my wife was born in Montreal and spoke perfect French and so forth. But she was the one, she said, 'you know, I prefer to go to-- to somewhere else. The political situation is not-- there is all this-- you know, this situation is not-- not so hot.' And the question is, I was at that time, no.

In Canada, I started off again as a cabinet maker. And I didn't get very far. I found out that there is no such thing, practically, in Canada, as custom made furniture. I had to work in a factory, and it is very poorly paid. So I took evening courses in architectural and mechanical and drafting, and I became a draftsman.

And eventually, in the late '50s-- in the middle '50s, in '56 or '55, I got a job with a-- with a consulting engineer, with a civil consulting engineer, as a draftsman. As a junior draftsman, then a draftsmen, then a senior draftsman, and so on and so forth. And that was much better, you know? Because there was no future in-- in being a-- being a cabinet maker or a carpenter.

INT: So you moved to Toronto after that?

HW: Yeah, in '69. In '60-- yeah, we bought a house eventually in [INAUDIBLE]. But then we sold the house, and in '69, we moved to Toronto. We bought another house. It's this house, which be bought in '69.

I'm still living here. And unfortunately, in '81, my wife died. She was a diabetic. She was a child diabetic and then it became-- the complications overtook her, and she-- she died, unfortunately, in-- in '81.

Then about a year later, I got married again. And my daughter eventually went to University in Ottawa and she decided to stay on in Ottowa, where she still is.

